"spell code" is a notebook page
	<header level="1">
		&lt;spell-code&gt;
		<header>A block of spell, coloured by spell's own parser.</>
	</>

	<text class="lede" paragraph>A <link target="src-packages-ui-docs-ui-code-html" link="/components/ui/code"><text code>&lt;ui-code&gt;</></> whose language is spell unless it says otherwise. Loading it registers spell with the page's Spell UI, so every <text code>&lt;ui-code language="spell"&gt;</> on the page is coloured too, and every spell block of a markdown.</>
	<list class="spell-meta">
		<item icon="user">Written for: anyone showing spell in a page, or changing how spell is coloured</>
		<item icon="play">Live: the real element, from the app's element build this page loads</>
	</>

	<section label="1. Example">
		<icon slot="icon" name="eye"/>
		<text paragraph>A snippet, and the same in a plain <text code>&lt;ui-code language="spell"&gt;</> beside it.</>
		<example "1. Example" description="`<spell-code>`:  spell, by default.">
			<html>
				<spell-code>
					<script type="text/plain">
						## Piles
						a pile is a list of cards
						the value of a pile is:
							return 0
					</script>
				</spell-code>
			</html>
		</example>
		<example "1. Example" description="Spell UI's `<ui-code language=\"spell\">`, coloured once `<spell-code>` has loaded.">
			<code language="spell">a card is a view</code>
		</example>
		<text class="meta" paragraph>Live, from <text code>/element/spell.pack.js</> (<text code>yarn build:element</> in <link target="src-spell-app" link="/"><text code>spell-app</></>), which this set's pages load.</>
	</>

	<section label="2. Using it">
		<icon slot="icon" name="code"/>
		<list bulleted>
			<item>Everything <text code>&lt;ui-code&gt;</> takes: inline text or a <text code>source</> file, <text code>line-numbers</>, <text code>wrap</>, <text code>copy</>, another <text code>language</>.</>
			<item><text code>spell/&lt;lang&gt;</> asks for a translation; one that doesn't exist stays plain, with a <text code>ui-error</>.</>
			<item>The colours come from spell's own parser, a lazy chunk fetched the first time a page shows spell.</>
			<item>Spell UI itself knows nothing of spell: this is one more language a page registers with <text code>UI.code.register()</>.</>
			<item>The docs pages (epic pages, guides) colour spell without it: the page server adds the app's <text code>spell-language.js</> to them, which registers the language and defines no element.</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="3. Attributes, events and texts">
		<icon slot="icon" name="list check"/>
		<text paragraph>Its own names; the rest are <link target="src-packages-ui-docs-ui-code-html" link="/components/ui/code"><text code>&lt;ui-code&gt;</></>'s, which it extends:</>
		<html>
			<ui-docs-api family="spell-code"></>
		</>
		<text class="meta" paragraph>From its vocabulary, by <text code>yarn site:data</> in <link target="src-spell-app" link="/"><text code>spell-app</></>.</>
	</>

	<section label="4. Its code">
		<icon slot="icon" name="folder open"/>
		<list bulleted>
			<item><link target="src-packages-app-components-spell-code-spellcode-tsx" link="/src/app/components/spell-code/SpellCode.tsx"><text title="src/app/components/spell-code/SpellCode.tsx" code>SpellCode.tsx</></>: its component, a <text code>UICode</></>
			<item><link target="src-packages-app-components-spell-code-spellcode-en-ts" link="/src/app/components/spell-code/SpellCode.en.ts"><text title="src/app/components/spell-code/SpellCode.en.ts" code>SpellCode.en.ts</></>: what its vocabulary adds to <text code>&lt;ui-code&gt;</>'s</>
			<item><link target="src-packages-app-components-spell-code-spelllanguage-ts" link="/src/app/components/spell-code/SpellLanguage.ts"><text title="src/app/components/spell-code/SpellLanguage.ts" code>SpellLanguage.ts</></>: the <text code>spell</> language it registers</>
			<item>Its build: <text code>yarn build:element</>, into <text code>dist-element/</>, served at <text code>/element/</></>
		</>
	</>
	<html>
		<footer class="site-page-footer">
